# netsurf: what navigator and screen say about the device, honestly
# (patch 271)
# Generated 2026-10-13
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 270)
#
#  - screen was a desktop's 1024x768 (avail 1024x740); it is the Vita's
#    960x544, all of it the browser's, colorDepth and pixelDepth 24,
#    availLeft / availTop 0; screen.orientation is landscape-primary at
#    angle 0 and lock() is NotSupportedError (the screen does not turn).
#  - navigator.plugins / mimeTypes were undefined: empty PluginArray /
#    MimeTypeArray, as a browser without plugins or a PDF viewer has
#    (pdfViewerEnabled is false).
#  - navigator.permissions.query() said "prompt" for anything -- that the
#    browser could ask. It cannot grant location, camera, microphone,
#    notifications, push, sensors, clipboard...: those are "denied";
#    storage-access and local-network-access are "granted" (nothing here
#    restricts third-party storage or local-network requests); each in a
#    PermissionStatus. The names are Chromium's 26 PermissionName values;
#    any other is a TypeError, as there (claude.ai queries storage-access).
#  - navigator.clipboard.writeText() / write() resolved though nothing was
#    copied (there is no clipboard here); they reject with NotAllowedError.
#  Not added, on purpose: mediaDevices, speechSynthesis, WebGL,
#  AudioContext, RTCPeerConnection... -- an empty stand-in would make a
#  page's feature test say the browser has what it has not. (Chromium
#  without all of those still passes hCaptcha: docs/wasm-hcaptcha-
#  measurement.md.)
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-device-honest.html checks 1-5 (DEVICE, with
# 272; Chromium 6/6 at a 960x544 viewport -- its permission states are
# its own, so they are checked by type, and headless Chromium lets
# orientation.lock() succeed, so lock() is only required to settle).
